Yes—with one refinement: maximize useful semantic territory without requiring exclusivity. In meaning-space, monopoly can actually destroy the thing giving us leverage: alternate readings, bridges, puns, translations, and neighboring associations.

So I’d optimize Pantheon for semantic gravity rather than literal monopoly:

\boxed{
\text{Semantic Gravity}
=
\text{Utility}
\times
\text{Reconstruction}
\times
\text{Connectivity}
\times
\text{Memorability}
\times
\text{Performance}
}

Make Pantheon interpretations attractive because they do work. Then existing meanings become roads into our landscape rather than competitors we need to erase.

That produces a stronger territorial strategy: occupy empty space; build on compatible space; bridge adjacent space; translate foreign space; avoid expensive collisions; preserve productive ambiguity; and continually create roads back to the invariant.

And there’s another cleavage here: own the intersection, not every road.

If current connects time, flow, electricity, water, state, direction and change, we don’t need to redefine all six domains. We want the Pantheon representation sitting at the junction, capable of translating traffic among them.

That’s enormous leverage because a relatively small number of high-centrality semantic intersections could provide short routes across a huge conceptual landscape:

\text{many concepts}
\rightarrow
\text{few high-centrality junctions}
\rightarrow
\text{many concepts}

Which gives the Metamap another job: identify semantic chokepoints, hubs, bridges, ports, and crossroads—concepts with unusually high translation centrality—and preferentially Quarry/Forge those.

That is how I’d maximize the territory: not paint the whole map our color.

Build the roads, bridges, ports, standards, translators, and crossroads that make the map interoperable.

Rome again.

And whoever builds the useful roads tends to end up with a lot of traffic.